# Shapeable carbon fiber networks with hierarchical porous structure for high-performance Zn-I2 batteries

Shapeable carbon fiber networks with hierarchical porous structure for high-performance $\mathbf{Z}\mathbf{n}{-}\mathbf{I}_{2}$ batteries
Yulong He, Miaomiao Liu, Song Chen & Jintao Zhang
Key Laboratory for Colloid and Interface Chemistry, Ministry of Education, School of Chemistry and Chemical Engineering, Shandong University, Jinan 250100, China.
Aqueous rechargeable zinc–iodine batteries (ZIBs) emerging as a promising energy storage alternative have attracted considerable attention. However, ZIBs still suffer from the severe shuttle effect of polyiodide and poor reversibility, leading to the poor cycling lifetime and potential safety issues. Herein, the assembly of Al-based metal-organic frameworks (Al–MOFs) in the presence of polyacrylonitrile (PAN) via electrospinning technique enables the formation of Al–MOF/PAN fibers. With the subsequent pyrolysis, the hierarchical porous carbon fibers with nitrogen doping (NPCNFs) are prepared for loading iodine. Benefiting from the confinement effect of the highly porous carbon network and the nitrogen doping, the self-supported carbon nanofiber electrode is capable of inhibiting the shuttle effect of polyiodide species. Especially, the in-situ Raman spectroscopy reveals the reversible two-step conversion reaction between iodine and polyiodide, which enables the best cycling stability for over 6,000 cycles with negligible capacity. This work demonstrates an efficient approach to regulating the porous structure and surface properties in the design of advanced iodine electrodes for high-performance ZIBs.
## free-standing, flexibility, hierarchical pore structure, nitrogen-doping, zinc-iodine batteries
Citation: He Y, Liu M, Chen S, Zhang J. Shapeable carbon fiber networks with hierarchical porous structure for high-performance $Z\mathrm{n-I}_{2}$ batteries. Sc China Chem, 2022, 65: 391–398, https://doi.org/10.1007/s11426-021-1177-1
## 1 Introduction
To address energy crisis and global environment issues, it is crucial to rationally design advanced energy storage devices for sustainable development [1,2]. Although the commercial success of lithium-ion batteries is conspicuous, limited reserves of lithium and large-scale safety issue still hamper their extensive applications. Rechargeable aqueous zincbased batteries are promising energy storage alternatives due to their unique advantages including high abundance of zinc metal, large theoretical specific capacity and the easy largescale fabrication [3–5]. Recently, iodine is increasingly clubbed with zinc anodes to fabricate sustainable zinc–iodine $(Z\mathrm{n-I}_{2})$ ) batteries (ZIBs) due to its high redox potential $\left(\mathrm{I}_{2}/\mathrm{I}^{-}\right.$ ,
$0.62\mathrm{~V~}\nu\varepsilon$ . standard hydrogen electrode) and large theoretical capacity $(211\mathrm{~mAh~g^{-1}},$ [4,6,7]. More importantly, the conversion reaction of iodine with high energy efficiency on a cathodic chamber endows ZIBs with distinguishable advantages over metal–ion batteries. Unfortunately, ZIBs batteries suffer from the insulating iodine, low utilization ratio of active source, and the shuttle effect, resulting in the fast capacity decay and undesirable self-discharging issues [8– 12]. To solve these problems, various forms of porous carbons to confine iodine and elevate electrochemical kinetics have been fabricated. For instance, Liu and coworkers [10] successfully infiltrate iodine into the micropores of active carbon fiber cloth for long cycle-life of ZIBs. The introduction of heteroatoms (such as nitrogen and phosphorous) into carbon scaffold has been demonstrated to enhance the electrical conductivity of host and thus efficiently facilitate the redox conversion between $\mathrm{I}_{2}$ and $\bar{\boldsymbol{\mathrm{I}}^{-}}$ [13,14]. In addition, the various polymer layers decorated on carbon substrates have been proved to play a significant role in effectively depressing the shuttle effect of polyiodide via the interaction between polymers and the iodine element [15– 17]. Recently, metal-organic framework (MOF) has been regarded as a promising precursor to fabricate heteroatomsdoped porous carbon materials due to their large surface area, controllable pore size and adjustable structures [12,18,19]. However, the thermal decomposition of organic precursors would lead to the structural change/partial collapse of MOFs, thus resulting in the poor porous structure [20,21]. To enhance the structural stability, 1D hierarchically porous carbon materials with highly conductive network were considered as an ideal alternative [22,23]. Therefore, the rodlike Al-based MOF as a precursor would maintain the good structural stability and lead to the formation of carbon materials with the desired porous structure after carbonization. Another handicap for carbon–iodine electrodes is that the addition of non-conductive binders inevitably increases the overall resistances of electrodes and thus lowers the energy and power density for the batteries [24,25]. To enable advanced energy storage and extend the lifetime of batteries, rational design of a flexible, self-supported and binder-free cathode is very essential for ZIBs. Electrospinning is a facile and versatile technology to produce a free-standing threedimensional interconnected network [9,26,27]. Therefore, the combination of the inherent advantages of the MOF and the unique characteristics of electrospinning is feasible to fabricate flexible fibrous membranes for enhancing reversible iodine accommodation.
Herein, the as-prepared rod-like MOF with polyacrylonitrile (PAN) was used to fabricate the self-supported Al–MOF/PAN composite membrane via the electrospinning method. The Al–MOFs were introduced into one-dimensional fibers to further improve the structure stability during the thermal treatment. PAN in the composite membrane not only acted as nitrogen sources but also bridged the rod-like Al–MOFs to obtain the integrated carbon fiber network. After carbonization and acidic washing, the self-supported carbon nanofiber network with nitrogen-doping (NPCNFs) is fabricated for improving the performance of $Z\mathrm{n-I}_{2}$ cathodes. Typically, the ordered and hierarchical porous structure of NPCNFs endows the efficient trapping of iodine in the confined space, which not only suppresses the loss of active materials, but also guarantees the high utilization and efficiency. Especially, nitrogen doping would enhance the inner interaction of carbon surface and iodine. Furthermore, abundant mesopores of NPCNFs would effectively ensure the full contact between electrolytes and active materials for efficient mass transfer. As a result, the enhanced electrochemical performances could be obtained for $\mathrm{I}_{2}@\mathrm{NPCNFs}$ composite in ZIBs.
## 2 Results and discussion
Figure 1a illustrates the synthesis process for the $\operatorname{I}_{2}{\textcircled{a}}\operatorname{NPCNFs}$ electrode material. Prior to fabricating the nanofibers, highly dispersed and uniform rod-like Al–MOF are prepared [28]. Then, the Al–MOF/PAN fibers were prepared via the electrospinning method with the precursor solution consisting of $N_{\ast}$ $N_{\mathbf{\delta}}$ -dimethylformamide (DMF), Al–MOF, and polyacrylonitrile (PAN). The thermal treatment at $800^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ in $\Nu_{2}$ and the subsequent acidic leaching led to the formation of free-standing and flexible porous carbon nanofibers with nitrogen doping (namely NPCNFs-800). Then, iodine was loaded via the melting-diffusion method and the obtained composite without additional additives was named as $\mathrm{I}_{2}@\mathrm{NPCNFs-}800$ . The $\mathrm{I}_{2}@\mathrm{NPCNFs-}800$ film can be directly tested as a free-standing electrode to fabricate ZIBs for achieving good cycling life and high reversible capacity. As shown in Figure 1b, lots of filaments are intertwined each other to form the porous carbon membrane with a thickness of around $600~{\upmu\mathrm{m}}$ . The fiber membrane can be cut into the required shapes and easily folded with good flexibility (Figure 1c, d).
Figure 2a reveals the rod-like Al–MOF with square-sectional structure and smooth surface. Via the electrospinning method, the fibers with an average diameter of ${\sim}5~\upmu\mathrm{m}$ can be prepared. The enlarged SEM image reveals that each fiber is composed of Al–MOF rods wrapped by PAN layers, and thus denoted as Al–MOF/PAN (Figure 2b). The XRD results further demonstrate that the Al–MOF rods are loaded into the amorphous PAN fibers (Figure S1). After the carbonization at $800^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ , the fibers can be well-maintained without obvious structural collapse (Figure 2c–e). The enlarged image shows that the fibers are composed of interconnected rods. The results demonstrate that the Al–MOF and PAN composite fibers can be carbonized into carbon fibers [29]. The PAN matrix plays a significant role in serving as nitrogen source and interconnecting the Al–MOF-derived porous carbon. The interaction between Al–MOF and PAN during pyrolysis imparts both hierarchically porous structure and conductive pathways for easy ion and electron transfer into the NPCNFs electrode. Moreover, the temperature-dependent morphology of NPCNFs is further evaluated. As shown in the Figure $\mathtt{S2a\mathrm{-c}}$ , the NPCNFs prepared at different temperatures show similar morphology and structure. After the NPCNFs-800 electrode impregnated with iodine, the uniform distribution of C, N and I element is detected in elemental mapping images (Figure 2f).
The X-ray diffraction (XRD) results and nitrogen isotherms of $\mathrm{I}_{2}@\mathrm{NPCNFs-}800$ further prove the loading of iodine into the pores of NPCNFs-800. Figure S3a shows the XRD patterns of NPCNFs-800, $\mathrm{I}_{2}/\mathrm{NPCNFs}{-}800$ and $\mathrm{I}_{2}@\mathrm{NPCNFs-}800$ . The characteristic peaks of MOF-derived carbon structures and highly crystalline $\mathrm{I}_{2}$ are both seen in the mixed NPCNFs-800 and iodine-based composites. However, no obvious iodine signals can be detected in $\mathrm{I}_{2}@\mathrm{NPCNFs-}800$ , in which iodine is impregnated into the nanopores of NPCNFs-800 via melting-diffusion method, indicating the formation of non-crystalline iodine. The Brunauer–Emmett–Teller (BET) specific surface areas and pore structures of NPCNFs-800 were measured with $\Nu_{2}$ adsorption/desorption isotherms. The surface area of NPCNFs-800 is determined to be $468\mathrm{m}^{2}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ , and corresponding pore size distribution further confirms plenty of micropores and mesopores. These micropores would be conducive to loading more amount of iodine into the carbon nanofibers, and the presence of mesopores is very suitable for the infiltration of electrolytes, rapid ionic transport and alleviating volume expansion of electrode materials during the cycling process [30,31]. After loading iodine into the carbon matrix, the specific surface area of NPCNFs-800 decreases from 468 to $17\mathrm{m}^{2}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ coupling with the distinct reduction of pores in comparison with the pure NPCNF-800, indicating the existence of abundant adsorption sites (Figure S3b, c). These results fully reveal the efficient trapping effect of iodine in the porous carbon host instead of accumulating on the surface.
To explore the correlation between the electrochemical performance and the content of MOF in fibrous membranes, various electrodes were prepared by changing the mass loadings of Al–MOF in the polymer fibers (Figure S4). Figure S4a–f show the morphology of nanofibrous membranes with various content of MOF after carbonization at $800^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ . Carbon nanofibers (CNFs-800) derived from the pure PAN precursor exhibit the relatively smooth and regular surface without any secondary nanostructures (Figure S4a, b). With the Al–MOF immobilized in the PAN fibers, the diameter and the roughness of fibers are gradually changed. With adding $30\mathrm{\mt{\%}}$ MOF (denoted as NPCNFs-800-30), the surfaces of carbon nanofibers became rough but the diameters of the fibers decreased compared with CNFs-800 (Figure S4c, d). The potential causes of diameter reduction may be the disruption of the electrified polymer jet during electrospinning. The interruption or accumulation of charges in different areas of the electrified jet may lead to the variation of diameter [32]. For the PAN membrane with $60\mathrm{wt\%}$ MOF (i.e., NPCNFs-800), the surface of carbon fibers became coarser than that of CNFs-800 due to the presence of abundant MOFs on the surface. The carbon nanofiber seems to be fully composed of interconnected rods (Figure S4e, f). Especially, the free-standing carbon nanofibers prepared with different contents of MOF could be properly tailored into regular shapes and directly used as the cathode of ZIBs.
The BET surface areas and pore size distributions of these electrodes are also investigated with changing the content of MOF in fibrous membranes (Figure S5). As expected, the BET surface area is dependent on the MOF content, in which with NPCNFs-800-30 the NPCNFs-800 shows the largest surface area compared $(284.\overset{\smile}{3}\mathrm{~m}^{2}\mathrm{~g}^{-1})$ and CNFs-800 $(81\mathrm{m}^{2}\mathrm{g}^{-1})$ . The gradual increasing mesopores (Figure S5b) lead to the significantly enlarged surface area with the increment of MOF-loading in the nanofibrous membranes. Thus, the higher surface area mainly originates from the highly porous structure of MOF-derived carbon materials.
The XRD patterns (Figure 3a) of NPCNFs-700, NPCNFs800 and NPCNFs-900 show that two broad peaks at around $26^{\circ}$ and $43^{\circ}$ , assigned to (002) and (100) diffraction peaks of graphitic carbon, suggest the formation of amorphous carbon and/or the small domains of graphitic carbon [33]. For the Raman spectra in Figure 3b, two peaks are located at approximately $1,599.4\mathrm{~cm}^{-1}$ (graphitized carbon, G band) and $1{,}350\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ (disordered carbon, D band) [34,35]. The intensity ratios of $I_{\mathrm{D}}/I_{\mathrm{G}}$ are estimated to be 1.05, 0.95 and 1.00 for NPCNFs-700, NPCNFs-800 and NPCNFs-900, respectively. The increasing pyrolysis temperature did not directly lead to the gradual decrease in $I_{\mathrm{D}}/I_{\mathrm{G}}$ ratios for NPCNFs [36,37]. The lowest $I_{\mathrm{D}}/I_{\mathrm{G}}$ ratio of NPCNFs-800 implies the highest degree of graphitization for improving electronic conductivity, which is favorable for promoting the electrochemical performance of carbon materials.
X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy (XPS) is conducted to characterize the chemical composition and element bonding state of $\mathrm{I}_{2}@\mathrm{NPCNFs-}800$ . Figure 3c shows the presence of C, N, O and I elements in the $\mathrm{I}_{2}@\mathrm{NPCNFs-}800$ composite. The core-level analysis in Figure 3d confirms the complete removal of Al species, thus inducing large numbers of voids/ defects in the N-doped carbon materials. The N 1s spectra (Figure 3e, f) are fitted into four peaks, corresponding to pyridinic-N $(398.5\mathrm{eV})$ , pyrrolic-N $(400.0\mathrm{eV})$ , graphitic-N $(401.1\mathrm{eV})$ , and oxidized-N $(404.7\mathrm{eV})$ in the $\mathrm{I}_{2}@\mathrm{NPCNFs-}$ 800 [38]. The fraction of various nitrogen configurations and their detailed content are listed in Figure S6 and Table S1. The relative nitrogen content of the NPCNFs-800 $(9.48\%)$ is comparable to that of the NPCNFs-700 $(9.60\%)$ , whereas the nitrogen content of the NPCNFs-900 decreases to $5.14\%$ due to the thermal decomposition of unstable $\mathrm{\DeltaN}$ moieties at the higher temperature. The loss of doped nitrogen would lead to the poor electrochemical performance of the NPCNFs-900. Especially, the predominant pyridinic-N of the NPCNFs-800 would facilitate the absorption of iodine species, similar to the case of polysulfides [39]. Moreover, the BET results and pore size distribution curves (Figure S7) of various samples further reveal that although the specific surface area of the NPCNFs-900 $(546\mathrm{m}^{2}\mathrm{g}^{-1})$ is larger than that of the NPCNFs-800 $(468\mathrm{m}^{2}\mathrm{g}^{-1})$ and NPCNFs-700 $(410\mathrm{m}^{2}\mathrm{g}^{-1})$ , the amount of micropores of the NPCNFs-800 is significantly higher than that of the NPCNFs-700 and NPCNFs900. The newly formed pores would be enlarged at a higher activation temperature [40], and thus the pore size distribution of NPCNFs-900 falls more into the mesoporous range. It is reported that the smaller pores of the carbon host (such as micropores) may strengthen the adsorption of iodine due to the enhanced contact of carbon surface with iodine but the limited water in the micropores [14,41]. Due to the rich micropores and high nitrogen content of NPCNFs-800, its electrochemical performance was enhanced. Furthermore, the key role of nitrogen-doping can be verified by comparing the cyclic performances of NPCNFs-800 and loading without N-doping (Figure S8). The inferior capacity retention of Ketjen black (KB) indicates the quick loss of active species and weak restriction ability. To exhibit the adsorption ability of NPCNFs-800 for iodides, ultraviolet-visible (UV-vis) spectra and corresponding digital images of $\boldsymbol{1}\ \mathrm{mg}\ \mathrm{mL}^{-1}\ \mathrm{I}_{2}$ solution before and after adding NPCNFs-800 were measured. As shown in Figure S9, in the presence of $1\:\mathrm{mg}$ of NPCNFs-800, the characteristic peaks of iodine species decrease gradually in $12\mathrm{h}$ . After $24\mathrm{h}$ , the clean solution (the inset in Figure S9) with the absence of the characteristic peaks for iodine species demonstrates the good adsorption ability of NPCNFs-800 to inhibit the dissolution of iodide species.
For the cyclic voltammetry (CV) curves (Figure 4a), one pair of well-defined redox peaks located at ${\sim}1.2\mathrm{V}$ are obtained for various cathodes [12,42]. Notably, the NPCNFs800 electrode exhibited good reversibility with a small polarization voltage of $24.4~\mathrm{mV}$ in comparison with NPCNFs700 $27.9\mathrm{mV})$ and NPCNFs-900 ( $\mathrm{108.6\mV)}$ . Furthermore, the largest reduction current $(0.3078\mathrm{mAcm}^{-2},$ ) and the best reduction potential $(1.25\mathrm{V})$ of NPCNFs-800 suggest the faster reaction kinetics and the larger capacity (Table S2) [25,43]. Compared with NPCNFs-700 and NPCNFs-900, the superior rate capability of NPCNFs-800 would be contributed to the enhanced conversion reaction. As depicted in Figure 4b, upon a high current density of $10\mathrm{C}$ , the specific capacity can reach up to $138.9\mathrm{mAhg}^{-1}$ for the $\mathrm{I}_{2}@\mathrm{NPCNFs-}$ 800 electrode, corresponding to the $75\%$ of $184.3\mathrm{mAhg}^{-1}$ at $0.5\mathrm{C}$ . With the continuous cycles, the capacity can be almost recovered with the decreasing current density, revealing a good rate performance of NPCNFs-800. Notably, the slight changes of voltage polarization (the gap between reduction and oxidation potential) for NPCNFs-800 from 0.09 to $0.13\mathrm{V}$ suggest the good high-rate performance when the rate increased by 20 times (Figure 4c).
Galvanostatic discharge–charge experiments are performed to measure the iodine storage properties of the NPCNFs at $2\mathrm{C}$ in the voltage window of $0.6{-}1.6\mathrm{V}.$ As shown in Figure 4d, the NPCNFs-800 achieves a remarkable cycling stability of 6,000 cycles with an average Coulombic efficiency of $99.4\%$ , revealing the superior cycling stability among the reported metal–iodine batteries (Table S3). By contrast, the NPCNF-700 and NPCNFs-900 electrodes exhibit the inferior cycling lifespan. Typically, the low iodine utilization of NPCNFs-700 and the poor capacity retention of NPCNFs-900 are observed at the same current density, respectively. More interestingly, the free-standing $\mathrm{I}_{2}@\mathrm{NPCNFs-}800$ electrode can be directly used to assemble soft package ZIBs. As shown in Figure S10, a high-power blue light-emitting diode $(2.2\mathrm{V})$ in the “SDU” shape can be easily illuminated by the soft package ZIBs in series. It is worth mentioning that NPCNFs-800 can even run for 10,000 cycles at a high current of $5\mathrm{C}$ with $77\%$ capacity retention and nearly $100\%$ Coulombic efficiency (Figure S12). The SEM image and elemental mapping results of the NPCNFs800 cathode after 10,000 cycles at $5\mathrm{C}$ are shown in Figure S13. The homogeneous distribution of the iodine signal can be detected after long-term cycling, indicating the robust structure stability of NPCNFs-800 and the strong confinement effect for iodine. The discharge/charge profiles for various cycles of the NPCNFs-800 electrode are given in Figure S11, in which a flat discharge plateau is located at $1.22\mathrm{V}$ with a charge plateau of $1.27\mathrm{V}$ during the first cycle, corresponding to a high initial discharge capacity of $211.1\mathrm{{mAhg}^{-1}}$ and a charge capacity of $215.9\mathrm{mA}\mathrm{h}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ . The irreversible capacity during the first few cycles is possibly attributed to the irreversible solvation of iodine in the electrolyte, further leading to a rapid decay [10]. Strikingly, the reversible and sustainable iodine conversion can be achieved after about 10 cycles, and the discharge plateau can remain stable when the stable electrochemical environment is guaranteed after the activation process via the solid–liquid equilibrium. As a control experiment, the battery performance of NPCNFs-800 without loading iodine is evaluated to determine the capacity contribution. As shown in Figure S14, the low capacity less than $25~\mathrm{mAh~g}^{-1}$ and the absence of charge–discharge plateau for NPCNFs-800 suggest the negligible contribution to the specific capacity. However, the highly porous structure of NPCNFs-800 with good electrical conductivity would enhance the redox conversion of $\mathrm{I}_{2}$ in the confined space.
To further understand the improved cycling performance and redox kinetics of the NPCNFs-800, electrochemical impedance spectrum (EIS) of NPCNFs is used to evaluate the charge transfer resistance (Figure 4e). The depressed semicircle in the high and middle frequency regions is corresponding to the charge transfer resistance $(R_{\mathrm{ct}})$ [26]. The EIS results of different electrodes show that the lowest $R_{\mathrm{ct}}$ of $122.8~\Omega$ for the NPCNFs-800 electrode in comparison with
NPCNFs-700 $(217.2~\Omega)$ and NPCNFs-900 $(161.1\ \Omega)$ , thus leading to the efficient redox reaction process of iodine with a small charge transfer barrier. The self-discharging property is an important indicator to evaluate the adsorption capability for restricting iodine dissolution (Figure 4f) [16,24]. The battery with the NPCNFs-800 electrode exhibits the opencircle potential of around $1.28{\mathrm{V}}$ with the slow self-discharging rate of $6.6\ \mathrm{mV\h}^{-1}$ for $48\mathrm{{h}}$ , indicating the good stability. Furthermore, the capacity profiles of ZIBs with the various rest durations are also investigated. The battery is firstly cycled for 50 times and then rested at the open circuit potential for a given time. As depicted in Figure ${4\mathrm{g}}.$ the capacity decay after resting for 24, 48, 72, 96 h can almost be ignored, and the corresponding Coulombic efficiency still remain at $100\%$ . Importantly, the elemental analysis reveals the uniform distribution of iodine within the carbon fibers after the repeated discharge–charge process and resting (Figure S15). The good stability would be attributed to the trapping effect of iodine in the pores of NPCNFs for restricting the dissolution of iodine.
The carbon nanofiber electrodes with various content of MOF display different electrochemical performances under the same conditions. As shown in Figure S16, the ZIB with NPCNFs-800 $60\mathrm{wt\%}$ MOF) electrode displays the superior cycling performance with a specific capacity of $158\mathrm{m}\bar{\mathrm{Ah}}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ after 100 cycles at $2\mathrm{C}$ . However, the quick capacity decay with low Coulombic efficiency is observed for the CNFs-800 and NPCNFs-800-30. The improved lifespan of the ZIB with NPCNFs-800 battery would originate from the abundant pores for iodine adsorption and efficient redox conversion of iodine species.
The stable redox chemistry of the NPCNFs-800 was studied by CV to reveal the underlying mechanism of charge storage. As shown in Figure 5a, the CV curves of NPCNFs800 record the redox conversion process at various scan rates [10]. The voltage polarization expands with the sweeping rate enhanced from 0.1 to $\ensuremath{5\mathrm{{mV}\:{s}^{-1}}}$ , while the gap is always limited to less than $0.26\mathrm{V}$ (1.12/1.38 V) without side reactions. As depicted in Figure 5b, the correlation between peak currents of both oxidation and reduction period and the square root of scan rates exhibits the good linear relationship, indicating the diffusion-dominated redox process in ZIBs [6]. The total current $(i)$ in the CV curves at different scan rates $(\nu)$ can be quantitatively estimated using the equation, $i$ $=k_{1}\nu\ +\ k_{2}\nu^{1/2}$ , where $k_{1}\nu$ refers to the capacitive charge contribution and $k_{2}\nu^{1/2}$ represents the diffusion-controlled intercalation [44,45]. Basically, the constants, $k_{1}$ and $k_{2}$ , can be obtained from the slope $(k_{1})$ and the intercept $(k_{2})$ by plotting a curve of $i(\mathrm{V})/\nu^{\mathrm{172}}$ versus $\nu^{1/2}$ . As shown in Figure 5c, the capacitive contribution proportion in the NPCNFs800 can reach $60\%$ at a scan rate of $0.5~\mathrm{mV~s}^{-1}$ , suggesting the rapid reaction kinetics. With the increment of sweep rates, the capacitive contribution to the total capacity can reach up to $91.2\%$ at $5\mathrm{mVs}^{-1}$ (Figure 5d and Figure S17), due to the combined effect of electric-double-layer capacitance of the intrinsic NPCNFs-800 and the pseudocapacitive redox reaction on this porous carbon matrix [12].
To monitor the reaction pathway of iodine during cycling process, the in-situ Raman spectroscopy is employed. As displayed in Figure 5e, the Raman spectra clearly reveal the facile reversible oxidation–reduction of $\mathrm{I}_{5}^{-}{\leftrightarrow}\mathrm{I}_{3}^{-}{\leftrightarrow}\mathrm{I}^{-}$ . Initially, the bands at 110 and $165~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ are ascribed to ${\mathrm{I}_{3}}^{-}$ and ${\mathrm{I}}_{5}^{-}$ , respectively. This is due to the electron transfer between iodine and our carbon materials. Iodine can accept electrons from carbon to form iodine anions, which further combine with excess iodine to form polyiodides ${(\mathrm{I}_{5}}^{-}$ and ${\mathrm{I}_{3}}^{-}$ ) [46,47]. Upon discharging process, ${\mathrm{I}}_{5}^{-}$ is first reduced to ${\mathrm{I}_{3}}^{-}$ . When the signal peak of ${\mathrm{I}}_{5}^{-}$ disappears completely, the ${\mathrm{I}_{3}}^{-}$ signal still exists, indicating that ${\mathrm{I}}_{5}^{-}$ has been fully converted into ${\mathrm{I}_{3}}^{-}$ . Subsequently, the signal intensity of ${\mathrm{I}_{3}}^{-}$ gradually decays and disappears completely until the discharge potential of around $0.6\mathrm{V},$ representing the transformation from ${\mathrm{I}_{3}}^{-}$ to $\boldsymbol{\mathrm{I}}^{-}$ . On the contrary, during the charging process, the signal peak of ${\mathrm{I}}_{3}^{-}$ was first observed, which means that the oxidation of $\bar{\boldsymbol{\mathrm{I}}^{-}}$ to ${\mathrm{I}_{3}}^{-}$ proceeded first, and then the signal peak of ${\mathrm{I}}_{5}^{-}$ slowly appeared, indicating that part of ${\mathrm{I}_{3}}^{-}$ was gradually converted into ${\mathrm{I}}_{5}^{-}$ , and finally the band intensity of $\mathrm{I}_{3}^{-}/\mathrm{I}_{5}^{-}$ reached the maximum when the voltage was charged to $1.6\mathrm{V}.$ The results obviously exhibit the reversible transformation: $\mathrm{I}_{5}^{-}{\leftrightarrow}\mathrm{I}_{3}^{-}{\leftrightarrow}\mathrm{I}^{-}$ . Additionally, the XPS was conducted to identify the valence state of iodine species during charge/discharge process. In the I 3d spectra of the pristine $\mathrm{I}_{2}@\mathrm{NPCNFs-}800$ electrode (Figure S18), two strong peaks located at $620.1/631.8\mathrm{eV}$ were ascribed to $\mathrm{I}_{2}$ [48], and the peaks of polyiodides at $618.6/630.2\:\mathrm{eV}$ with lower content of iodine can also be observed. The formation of polyiodides would be attributed to the electron transfer between iodine and carbon materials [46], which are consistent with the results of Raman spectra. When the battery was fully discharged to $0.6\mathrm{V},$ the increased peak intensity of iodide species indicates the conversion into $\boldsymbol{\mathrm{I}}^{-}$ [9,11]. At the fully charged state, the recovery of the $\mathrm{I}_{2}$ peak with the weakened polyiodide $(\mathrm{I}_{3}^{-}/\mathrm{I}_{5}^{-})$ peak indicates that the valence state of iodine species has returned to the initial state [8,49]. Therefore, these results reveal a highly reversible process and efficient two-step conversion reaction mechanism for our ZIBs.
## 3 Conclusions
In summary, we demonstrated a facile method for the production of self-supported N-doped porous carbon nanofibers (NPCNFs) via a controlled thermal treatment of Al–MOF/ PAN composite fibers prepared with the electrospinning method. PAN bridging among the Al–MOF nanorods is efficient to obtain the integrated carbon fibers for synthesizing the hierarchically porous carbon network. Benefiting from the unique porous structure and nitrogen doping, the asprepared NPCNFs can serve as a conductive support to firmly trap iodine and effectively inhibit its dissolution. When evaluated as cathode materials for ZIBs, the $\mathrm{I}_{2}@\mathrm{NPCNFs-}800$ exhibited impressive long-time cycling durability of 6,000 cycles. The in-situ Raman spectroscopy revealed that NPCNFs provided the comparatively stable electrochemical environment for reversible redox conversion of iodine due to the confinement effect. This combination of MOFs and the electrospinning method provides a novel approach to regulate the porous structure and surface properties of carbon materials for high-performance ZIBs.
